```javascript
function switchAdjectiveOrder(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into words and identify adjectives using a part-of-speech tagger or a list of common adjectives
  const words = sentence.split(' ');
  const adjectives = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    const posTag = getPartOfSpeechTag(words[i]); // Replace getPartOfSpeechTag with an actual part-of-speech tagging library or your own logic for identifying adjectives

    if (posTag && posTag.startsWith('JJ')) { // Assuming 'JJ' is the part-of-speech tag for adjectives
      adjectives.push(words[i]);
      words.splice(i--, 1); // Remove the identified adjective from the original array
    }
  }

  // Switch the order of adjectives and join them back with other words
  const switchedAdjectives = [];
  for (let j = adjectives.length - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
    switchedAdjectives.push(adjectives[j]);
  }

  for (const word of words) {
    if (!switchedAdjectives.includes(word)) {
      switchedAdjectives.push(word);
    }
  }

  return switchedAdjectives.join(' ') + ' ' + words[words.length - 1]; // Add the last word if it's not already an adjective
}

// Example usage
const sentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
console.log(switchAdjectiveOrder(sentence)); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

This function uses an idealized `getPartOfSpeechTag` method which you would need to replace with an actual part-of-speech tagging library or your own logic for identifying adjectives in a sentence. This will make the function more accurate and robust for handling various sentences.

